title: SaaS Security & SSPM
page: saas-security.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: A modern SaaS security program runs SSPM for posture, CASB for inline DLP, ITDR for identity detection, and a recurring OAuth app review no tool does for you.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
The average organization runs 200+ SaaS apps, and about a third are bought without IT's knowledge. Each holds identities, data and OAuth grants, and most run on vendor defaults written for adoption, not security.
notes:
The page puts the average organization at 200+ SaaS apps, and about a third of them are bought without IT's knowledge. That third is shadow IT. Each app holds identities, data and OAuth grants, and most of them are configured to the vendor's defaults, which are written for adoption rather than security.

SSPM, SaaS Security Posture Management, is the configuration-posture management layer for SaaS. It is the third leg of the posture-management stool, alongside CSPM for IaaS and KSPM for Kubernetes.

A modern SaaS security program runs SSPM for posture, CASB for inline DLP and shadow-IT discovery, ITDR for identity-layer detection, and a recurring OAuth app review process that no tool does for you.

The work tiers naturally. Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, the IdP, GitHub and Salesforce are the Tier 0 systems whose compromise is an extinction event. Everything else gets baselined as the program matures.

== SaaS sprawl in one 2026 benchmark
layout: stats
section: problem
stat: 305 | SaaS apps on average | One 2026 benchmark
stat: 240 | SaaS apps at the median | The same benchmark
stat: 34% | Bought without IT's knowledge | Shadow IT, in the same benchmark
Any employee with a credit card and 60 seconds can stand up a workspace and pipe sensitive data through it before anyone in security knows it exists.
notes:
Two numbers frame the discipline. The first: the average organization runs 200+ SaaS applications, 305 on average and 240 at the median in one 2026 benchmark, and the count has held steady while spend still rose. The second: about a third of them, 34% in the same benchmark, are bought without IT's knowledge, on a corporate card, with a work email, or through a free-tier link in someone's Slack DM.

The page calls that gap the structural reality of modern work rather than a hygiene problem. SaaS is too easy to adopt. The vendor's incentive is frictionless onboarding, and the customer's discovery problem is the externality.

The data inside those apps is not a side concern. The customer list lives in Salesforce, the source code in GitHub, payroll and PII in Workday, and the strategy decks in Google Drive and SharePoint. Most enterprises have more sensitive data in SaaS than in their cloud IaaS estate, yet the cloud security program is often heavily IaaS-weighted, because that is where the discipline grew up.

Every app also has its own admin console, permission model and audit log shape. SaaS security is breadth work across many systems, shallow at first and deepening on the Tier 0 ones, where IaaS security is depth work in three near-identical cloud APIs.

== What shifts from IaaS to SaaS
layout: table
section: model
row: Layer | IaaS (yours) | SaaS (yours)
row: OS / runtime / app | You | Provider
row: Identity and access | You (IAM) | You (SSO, MFA, SCIM, OAuth grants)
row: Tenant configuration | You (resource configs) | You (settings, sharing, retention)
row: Logging | You | You; access varies wildly by app and license
row: Third-party apps | N/A directly | You (OAuth grants, marketplace apps)
row: Incident response | Full forensic access | Limited by vendor cooperation, log retention
Your vendor's incident is now your incident, and log access is licensed per app and tier.
notes:
The shared responsibility model tilts dramatically as you move from IaaS to SaaS. In SaaS the vendor owns the operating system, the runtime, the application, the network and the physical infrastructure. What is left to you is narrower but no less important. This is an excerpt of the page's table, which also lists data classification and DLP as yours in both models.

Two consequences follow. Every row where the answer is you needs a process, an owner and a control, and there is no IaaS-style API uniformity to ride on. And the rows where the answer is the vendor are not free. A breach at Okta, Snowflake, GitHub, Twilio, MOVEit, Cloudflare, Microsoft or any major SaaS becomes a same-day investigation for every customer, so third-party risk management turns from a once-a-year questionnaire into a continuous monitoring problem.

Log access is licensed. Salesforce Event Monitoring requires Shield, Slack Audit Logs require Enterprise Grid, and Microsoft 365 audit logs at the Purview Advanced tier and the entry-level tier differ by months of retention and depth of fields. Security teams often find out about these license gates during an incident, which is the worst time to discover them. Inventory the per-app log entitlements during program design, not during an investigation.

== What SSPM is, and what it is not
layout: compare
section: sspm-def
col: What it reads, per app | Users, admin roles and authentication settings | Sharing and the data exposure surface | OAuth apps, their scopes and who granted them | Audit log setup and risky vendor defaults
col: What it is not | A CASB: it sits outside the data path | An IdP, or a DLP product | An ITDR: detection is ITDR's job | A vendor risk management tool
SSPM continuously assesses SaaS configuration through each app's admin APIs and reports findings against a baseline: the CSPM model, on entirely different APIs.
notes:
SSPM is the discipline of continuously assessing the security configuration of SaaS applications through their administrative APIs and emitting findings against a baseline. The model is a direct lift-and-shift from CSPM: connect to the admin API, enumerate the tenant, evaluate against rules, surface drift.

Per connected app it reads users and admin roles, including who has not logged in for 90 days and who has MFA enabled; authentication settings; sharing and collaboration; every installed OAuth app with its scopes, the users who granted it and when it was last used; public and externally shared data; audit log configuration; and tenant-specific risky defaults, such as anonymous SharePoint link sharing or GitHub default repo visibility.

It does not sit in the data path or inspect uploads and downloads for DLP. It reads the IdP's settings and consumes its logs, but does not authenticate users. It is preventive, while detection such as impossible travel, MFA fatigue and session-token theft is ITDR's job. Some SSPMs surface public files, but they do not classify content beyond what the SaaS already labels. And SSPM tells you about apps you run, while vendor risk management is about whether the vendor can be trusted.

The rules cannot be transferred from CSPM. The page says a CSPM that also does SaaS is, in practice, almost always thinner on the SaaS side than a dedicated SSPM, particularly for Salesforce permission sets and Slack workspace fragmentation.

== The four pillars of SaaS security
layout: table
section: pillars
row: Pillar | What it covers
row: Identity and access | SSO for every app, SCIM, MFA, dormant accounts, tiered admins, OAuth reviews
row: Configuration posture | Admin role sprawl, public sharing, external collaboration, risky vendor defaults
row: Data security | DLP, public files, labels, retention, e-discovery and legal hold
row: Threat detection | Anomalous logins, malicious OAuth apps, mass downloads, exfil via API
Most SaaS breaches are identity breaches first and configuration breaches second. Programs that skip one pillar pay for it in the others.
notes:
The page says every SaaS security program reduces to four interlocking pillars, and weakness in any one of them undermines the others.

Identity and access is the bedrock: SSO coverage of every app including the long tail, SCIM so leavers lose access, MFA enforced including on break-glass paths, dormant accounts pruned on a cadence, admin roles tiered with Privileged Access Management, and OAuth grants reviewed regularly.

Configuration posture is the CSPM-shaped work of SSPM. Concrete examples: M365 anonymous SharePoint link sharing turned off, Salesforce guest-user object permissions audited, Slack workspace-app install restricted to admins, and GitHub org default repo visibility set to private.

Data security is the pillar where compliance and security overlap most directly: DLP on the SaaS that hold sensitive data, public files surfaced and reviewed, sensitive data in chat, wiki and CRM labelled, retention set per regulatory requirement, and e-discovery and legal hold that work.

Threat detection lives in the ITDR and SIEM layer over the SaaS audit logs. The page calls it the pillar most often discovered missing during an incident.

The pillars compound. Strong identity makes posture drift less catastrophic, strong posture reduces the blast radius of identity compromise, strong data controls reduce the consequence of either failing, and strong detection catches everything that the first three missed.

== What a consented OAuth token keeps
layout: figure
section: oauth
figure: oauth-token
A user clicks Allow on a consent screen, and the third-party app gets a refresh token that, depending on the platform, may never expire.
notes:
The mechanism: a user clicks a Sign in with Google or Microsoft button on a third-party app, the SaaS shows a consent screen requesting scopes such as read your email or manage your repositories, the user clicks Allow, and the app gets a refresh token that, depending on the platform, may never expire and may bypass conditional-access policies on later uses.

The figure lists five properties of that token. There is no MFA prompt on later use, because the user already authenticated when they granted consent. It often has no expiry: refresh tokens can persist indefinitely until revoked. It survives password changes, so resetting a compromised user's password does not invalidate their grants. It is often invisible: most users cannot list the apps they have authorized, and many admins cannot either without an SSPM or the native admin console. And when the app has admin consent, some grants apply to the entire org instead of the granting user alone.

The page also names the compromised integration as a related risk. In the 2022-2023 GitHub OAuth-token incidents, attackers stole the tokens held by the Heroku, Travis CI and CircleCI integrations and used them against those vendors' customers. Each showed that a single compromised vendor with broad OAuth scopes can reach many customer organizations at once.

== Defending against OAuth abuse
layout: checklist
section: oauth
OAuth consent has functionally replaced credential phishing as the higher-value attack path in many recent campaigns. The attacker needs only a click on Allow.
check: Require admin consent; no user grants for non-trivial scopes
check: Keep a continuous inventory of apps, scopes, grantors and last use
check: Tier apps by scope impact; review high-impact apps quarterly
check: Practice revoking tokens at scale within an hour
check: Alert on new grants, particularly with mail, drive or admin scopes
notes:
The page calls OAuth app authorization the structural SaaS risk most under-managed by security teams in 2026. The attacker does not need the user's password or MFA token; they need the user to click Allow on a convincing-looking consent screen.

The defensive posture every program needs starts with admin consent. M365, Google Workspace and most modern SaaS allow restricting user grants so net-new apps require admin approval, and the page says the default everywhere should be no user-driven OAuth grants for non-trivial scopes.

Keep a continuous inventory of authorized apps, their scopes, the users who granted them and last-used timestamps; without it you have no idea what is connected. Tier the inventory by impact, since read-email and write-email scopes are higher risk than calendar-read, and review the high-impact apps quarterly.

When a vendor breach is disclosed, and the page expects that to happen frequently, you need to be able to revoke tokens at scale in M365, Workspace, GitHub, Slack and Salesforce within an hour. Practice it. And alert on new OAuth app grants in the IdP audit log, particularly grants with mail, drive or admin scopes.

The page names the vendors most active here: Push Security, Valence Security, Reco, Obsidian, AppOmni, Adaptive Shield (now CrowdStrike Falcon Shield) and Spin.AI, which all centre OAuth-app inventory and risk-scoring as a primary feature. Its advice: if you are evaluating SSPM and OAuth is a third-class feature, evaluate something else.

== The best discovery programs fuse four signals
layout: table
section: shadow-it
row: Source | What it catches
row: DNS and network egress (SSE / SASE) | Every SaaS domain a managed user reaches
row: SSO and finance reconciliation | Apps the org pays for but has not integrated
row: Email and calendar parsing | Apps with neither network nor finance signal
row: Inline CASB or browser extension | Per-session view; most invasive, highest resolution
No single signal catches everything. A first pass typically finds 2-3x more SaaS apps in use than the official catalogue claims.
notes:
You cannot secure what you cannot enumerate. The page treats shadow IT discovery in 2026 as a multi-source reconciliation problem, and says the best programs fuse four signals.

SSE and SASE products such as Zscaler, Netskope, Cisco Secure Access, Palo Alto Prisma Access and Cloudflare One see every SaaS domain a managed user reaches, and traffic volume gives a usage signal. The IdP catalog lists the SaaS the org has officially integrated, and finance data lists what it pays for. The delta between the two is high-signal shadow IT, often the most operationally important kind. Parsing password-reset emails, calendar invites and vendor onboarding emails surfaces apps with neither network nor finance signal. Inline CASB and browser-extension telemetry is the most invasive source but also the highest-resolution.

The reconciliation produces one inventory keyed by app, with how each app was discovered, how many use it, what the org spends on it, and whether it has SSO. The typical first pass finds 2-3x more apps than the official catalogue claims, and at least a handful of Tier-0-data apps, typically a file-sync, survey or marketing-automation tool, running outside IT's awareness.

Discovery is the easy half. The hard half is what you do with the list: onboarding apps to SSO and SCIM, negotiating enterprise tenancy with vendors that started as employee sign-ups, and deciding what to retire, consolidate or leave alone. That work is operational, and it decides whether the inventory gets used or sits in a dashboard.

== SSPM vs CASB
layout: table
section: sspm-vs-casb | casb-platforms
row: Dimension | CASB | SSPM
row: Deployment | Inline proxy and / or API | API-only, out of band
row: Primary function | Inline DLP, access control, shadow-IT block or coach | Configuration assessment, OAuth inventory, admin role audit, drift
row: Sees | Traffic and file content in flight | Tenant configuration, app integrations, user inventory
row: Misses | Internal reconfiguration drift, admin-only changes | User behavior in session, data in and out
row: Best for | Real-time DLP and access policy | Posture and configuration governance
CASB sits in the data path; SSPM sits outside it. Mature programs run both.
notes:
The page says the two categories are confused frequently, and gives the simplest distinction: CASB sits in the data path, and SSPM sits outside it. CASB is a 2012-era category that matured into part of SSE and SASE; SSPM is a 2019-era category that matured into a distinct discipline.

They overlap on API-mode posture features. Most CASBs have an API mode that reads tenant configuration, and some SSPMs are starting to add lightweight DLP through the SaaS's native APIs. The primary value propositions remain distinct. Mature programs run both, and increasingly from different vendors, because the depth of SSPM coverage in a CASB rarely matches a focused SSPM, and vice versa.

One emerging consolidation pattern is SSE platforms with bolted-on SSPM. The page frames the trade-off as a 70%-good integrated suite against a 95%-good best-of-breed pair, and says to pick it knowingly.

The CASB category itself is mature and largely consolidated into the SSE platforms. If you are starting from zero in 2026, the page's practical recommendation is to pick the SSE or SASE your network team has already chosen, take its CASB module, and add a dedicated SSPM if the diversified-SaaS depth matters. Standalone CASB without an SSE wrapper has largely stopped being a viable category.

== How to evaluate an SSPM
layout: checklist
section: platforms
Every SSPM lists 100+ supported apps. Ask how many checks it runs on M365, Workspace, Salesforce, GitHub and Slack.
check: Depth on Tier 0 and Tier 1 apps against a long-tail app you need
check: OAuth-app inventory and risk-scoring, the likeliest week-one value
check: Connecting M365 and Workspace should take 30 minutes each
check: Findings reach Jira or ServiceNow and the SIEM, with context
check: Mapping to SOC 2, ISO 27001, NIST CSF and CIS benchmarks
notes:
The page says the category consolidated significantly in 2024-2025. It lists the major dedicated vendors: Adaptive Shield (acquired by CrowdStrike in 2024), AppOmni, Obsidian Security, With Wings, Grip Security, Reco, Valence Security, Suridata, Spin.AI, Push Security and Atomicwork, and native or platform offerings: Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps, Cisco Cloudlock, and the SSPM modules inside the Netskope, Palo Alto and Zscaler SSE platforms.

To evaluate, start with depth on your Tier 0 and Tier 1 apps. Every SSPM lists 100+ supported apps, so the relevant question is how many checks it runs on M365, Workspace, Salesforce, GitHub and Slack, and how that compares with the same vendor's coverage of a long-tail app you also need.

OAuth-app inventory and risk-scoring is the feature most likely to deliver value in week one. For time-to-value, connecting to M365 and Workspace should be 30 minutes each; if onboarding a Tier 0 app takes weeks, the vendor's integration depth is shallow.

Findings need to flow to your ticketing system and your SIEM, with the per-finding context the assignee needs. And look for framework mapping to SOC 2, ISO 27001 and NIST CSF, plus per-app benchmarks such as CIS Microsoft 365 Foundations, CIS Google Workspace and CIS Salesforce.

== Not every SaaS deserves the same investment
layout: figure
section: priority-saas
figure: saas-tiers
A Tier 0 compromise, or its vendor's, is a same-day, all-hands event, so per-app deep work there pays off disproportionately.
notes:
The figure shows the page's four tiers, with coverage flowing from the top down.

Tier 0 is the extinction-event apps: Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, the IdP (Okta, Entra ID, Ping or JumpCloud), Salesforce, GitHub or GitLab, and the secrets manager. Tier 1 is the major systems of record, such as Atlassian, ServiceNow, Workday, Slack or Teams, Zoom, Box or Dropbox, Snowflake and Databricks. Tier 2 is operational SaaS such as HubSpot, Marketo, Zendesk, Intercom, DocuSign and the BI, survey and marketing-stack tools. Tier 3 is the long tail: everything else, baselined to SSO, SCIM and MFA and not deeply audited.

A program that has not baselined Tier 0 has no business spending time on Tier 3. The Tier 0 systems share a property: their compromise, or their vendor's compromise, is a same-day, all-hands event. That is why their per-app deep work, such as Salesforce permission sets, GitHub branch protection and M365 Conditional Access, pays off disproportionately.

== Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace
layout: table
section: m365 | workspace
row: Control | Microsoft 365 | Google Workspace
row: Posture dashboard | Secure Score, the floor | Security Center
row: Access policy | Conditional Access | Context-Aware Access
row: Sharing | Turn anonymous links off; override per site | Restrict to internal-only; override per folder
row: OAuth apps | App consent policies | Allowed apps only; admin approval
row: Baselines | CIS; CISA SCuBA with ScubaGear | CIS; CISA SCuBA with ScubaGoggles
For most enterprises M365 is the highest-stakes SaaS tenant, and licensing decides which controls you have.
notes:
M365 is, for most enterprises, the highest-stakes SaaS tenant in the estate, and the licensing structure determines which controls you have access to. Secure Score is the native posture dashboard: useful as a baseline, but shallow compared with a dedicated SSPM. Conditional Access decides who can authenticate from where, on what device and under what conditions, and the page calls it the most important control surface in M365. Around it sit Defender for Office 365 for anti-phishing, Safe Links and Safe Attachments; Defender for Cloud Apps; Defender for Identity; and Purview for labels, DLP, retention and e-discovery.

Anonymous link sharing is the most common public-data exposure in any M365 tenant, and the default in many tenants still allows it. Default it off and override per site where there is a business justification. Exchange external sender rules, mail-forwarding restrictions and OAuth app consent policies are three quick wins.

Workspace's security model is more uniform, with fewer products, more consolidated under the admin console, but the discipline is similar. Security Center is the posture dashboard, Alert Center is the detection feed to forward to your SIEM, and Context-Aware Access is the Conditional Access equivalent, combined with Chrome Enterprise Premium for full Zero Trust enforcement. Enforce 2-Step Verification, including hardware keys for admins and high-risk users, and configure Vault retention before you need it.

Both have CIS benchmarks and CISA SCuBA baselines. SCuBA ships open-source assessment scripts for M365, ScubaGear, which the page says produce evidence the auditors will accept, and ScubaGoggles for assessing Workspace.

== Salesforce rewards specialization
layout: checklist
section: salesforce
The sharing model is nuanced enough that a generalist SSPM check is rarely sufficient.
check: Treat Health Check as the floor, not the ceiling
check: Shield for regulated industries: Event Monitoring, Field Audit Trail, encryption
check: Permission Set Groups and a regular review cadence
check: Audit guest-user object permissions on every Experience Cloud site
check: Restrict Connected App installs; require admin approval
check: Verify MFA, particularly for API users and integration accounts
notes:
The page says Salesforce is the SaaS whose security model rewards specialization. Its sharing model of org-wide defaults, role hierarchies, sharing rules, manual shares, account and opportunity teams, public groups and queues is nuanced enough that a generalist SSPM check is rarely sufficient.

Health Check is the native posture score: floor, not ceiling. Salesforce Shield is a paid add-on covering Event Monitoring, the high-fidelity audit log, plus Field Audit Trail, Platform Encryption and Einstein Data Detect. For any regulated industry the page calls Shield functionally required.

Most production orgs accumulate years of permission-set sprawl, and the page says Permission Set Groups plus a regular review cadence is the only known cure.

Guest-user controls on Experience Cloud and Communities sites were historically a source of major data exposures, with multiple high-profile incidents in 2020-2023. Audit guest-user object permissions, sharing rules and Apex class access on every site.

Connected Apps are the OAuth inventory in Salesforce vocabulary: restrict who can install them, require admin approval, and audit IP restrictions on their tokens. Partner portals, Experience Cloud sites and Salesforce-to-Salesforce are each a way data can leave the primary sharing model. MFA has been required by Salesforce since 2022, so verify enforcement, particularly for API users and integration accounts.

== GitHub and GitLab: the non-negotiables
layout: checklist
section: github
Your code, CI / CD secrets and deployment keys live here, often with a path to production cloud accounts via OIDC.
check: Enforce SAML / SSO at the org or group level; add SCIM
check: Hardware-key MFA for every member, especially admins
check: Branch protection on every default branch
check: Secret scanning with push protection
check: Review third-party OAuth apps quarterly; revoke unused ones
check: OIDC federation instead of PATs wherever the cloud supports it
check: Ship the audit log to the SIEM
notes:
For engineering organizations, the page calls the source-code system the SaaS whose compromise is closest to an extinction event: your code, your CI / CD secrets, your deployment keys, and often a path to your production cloud accounts via OIDC.

Without enforced SSO, ex-employees may retain personal-account access to org repos, and SCIM ties membership to your IdP so leavers are removed automatically. Hardware-key MFA covers every member, particularly accounts with org-admin or repo-admin rights, and GitHub Enterprise allows enforcement. Branch protection means required reviews, status checks, no force pushes and no direct pushes to main. Secret scanning catches credentials before they land in the repo, and push protection blocks the push instead of detecting it after. Add dependency scanning such as Dependabot or Renovate, and put code scanning such as CodeQL, Semgrep or Snyk Code in the default branch's required checks.

CI / CD vendors, IDE integrations and code-analysis tools all request broad GitHub scopes, so audit the org's authorized apps quarterly. Personal Access Tokens are long-lived secrets and a frequent leakage source, so wherever the cloud target supports OIDC federation, use that instead. The audit log is the evidence trail for every code, secret and Actions event; forward it.

The same principles apply to GitLab and Bitbucket. The brand names differ; the discipline is the same.

== Slack and Teams
layout: checklist
section: slack-teams
Chat holds incident bridges, deal negotiations and salary discussions. The first scan of an organization's Slack typically finds AWS keys, Stripe keys and customer records in DMs.
check: Run DLP for secrets, PII and customer data in messages
check: Restrict app installs to admins; approve new requests
check: Default Slack Connect and Shared Channels off; approve per channel
check: Set retention per channel and workspace; forever is rarely right
check: SSO, SCIM and dormant-account pruning, as for other Tier 0 and Tier 1 apps
check: Forward audit logs to the SIEM
notes:
Chat platforms are where the most sensitive ad-hoc conversations happen: incident bridges, deal negotiations, salary discussions and customer escalations. The page says their security controls are often underconfigured relative to the data they hold.

Slack DLP, Microsoft Purview Communication Compliance and SSPM-side DLP all surface secrets, PII and customer data in messages. Slack workspace apps and Teams apps each have their own marketplace and consent model, so restrict who can install apps to admins. Slack Connect and Teams Shared Channels make external collaboration trivial, and easy to misuse: default to off and allow per channel with approval.

Retention policies should align with regulatory and legal-hold requirements. The default in many tenants is forever, which the page says is rarely the right answer. E-discovery through the Slack Discovery API and the Teams Compliance Center is required for litigation hold and regulated industries.

Slack EKM and Teams Customer Key let customers hold the keys and are required for the most regulated tenants, but they are operationally heavy, so do not adopt them without a use case. Slack Audit Logs require Enterprise Grid, and the M365 Unified Audit Log covers Teams. Forward both.

== ITDR: detection over identity and SaaS logs
layout: list
section: itdr
Where SSPM tells you the configuration is wrong, ITDR tells you an identity is being abused.
item: Impossible travel and geo anomalies
item: MFA fatigue, the Uber 2022 pattern
item: Session-token theft, the AiTM phishing pattern
item: New OAuth grants with high-impact scopes
item: Privilege escalation, such as roles granted outside the workflow
item: Golden SAML and cross-tenant trust abuse
item: Mass-download and mass-export events
notes:
ITDR is the detection-and-response layer over the identity provider and the SaaS audit logs, the SOC-side counterpart to SSPM's preventive posture.

Impossible travel is classic but still relevant when adversaries forget about residential-proxy hygiene. MFA fatigue is repeated pushes that eventually get approved by a frustrated user. Session-token theft is a token used from a different device fingerprint than where it was issued. A new OAuth grant with high-impact scopes, particularly mail.read or files.readwrite.all, is often the first observable of a tenant-takeover campaign. Mass export means, for example, a user downloading 10,000 Salesforce records in an hour, exporting an entire SharePoint site, or cloning every repo in the org. The page also lists anomalous behavior from a long-static service account.

The data sources are the IdP logs, the M365 Unified Audit Log, the Workspace Reports API, Salesforce Event Monitoring (which requires Shield), the GitHub Audit Log, and Slack Audit Logs (Enterprise Grid only).

The model the page says works in practice: SSPM for the preventive baseline, ITDR for the live detection, both feeding a SIEM that the Cloud SOC watches with IR playbooks per Tier 0 app.

== Compliance: your estate and your vendors
layout: checklist
section: compliance
SaaS compliance splits into two halves: what you owe your customers about your SaaS estate, and what you ensure your vendors deliver to you.
check: A current SOC 2 Type II or equivalent from every Tier 0 SaaS
check: A signed BAA covering the specific service, for any vendor handling PHI
check: Know where data and backups live, and whether you can constrain the region
check: Track and review sub-processor lists
check: DPAs and SCCs for cross-border data flows
check: Breach notification SLAs that fit your own regulatory clock
notes:
The page says every Tier 0 SaaS should produce a current SOC 2 Type II or equivalent, and that the TSC scope matters: Security plus Confidentiality is the minimum for most data-handling vendors. Review annually and track expirations.

A Business Associate Agreement is required for any vendor that handles PHI. Verify the signed BAA and that the specific service in scope is on the vendor's BAA-eligible list, since both M365 and Workspace have feature subsets that fall outside the BAA boundary.

GDPR, China PIPL, India DPDP and several other regimes have hard residency requirements that the SaaS may not natively enforce. GDPR requires that the customer be notified of sub-processor changes, and CCPA and state-privacy regimes have similar mechanics. DPAs and Standard Contractual Clauses are the contractual layer for cross-border flows, refreshed as case law and adequacy decisions evolve. Most SaaS now self-publish due-diligence artefacts through trust portals such as Whistic, SafeBase or OneTrust Vendorpedia.

Finally, vendor contracts should commit them to notify you within a defined timeframe, so you can meet your own regulatory clock: 72 hours under GDPR.

== Building a SaaS security program
layout: timeline
section: program
event: Discover | One inventory from SSO, finance, DNS / SSE and email parsing
event: Enrich and tier | Owner, data class, SSO, SCIM, MFA status; Tier 0 to 3
event: Baseline and remediate | SSPM against CIS or SCuBA; work the findings backlog
event: Govern OAuth | Admin consent policies, review cadence, revocation playbook
event: Monitor and detect | Findings to ticketing; Tier 0 audit logs to the SIEM
event: IR and lifecycle | IR playbook per Tier 0 app; onboarding and offboarding workflows
This is a 12-18-month program for a typical enterprise, and it does not finish: SaaS sprawl is monotonic, so the work is permanent.
notes:
The page sequences the work in ten steps, grouped here into six.

Discovery reconciles SSO, finance, DNS or SSE and email parsing into a single inventory; expect to find 2-3x more apps than IT believed it ran. Inventory enrichment records per app the owner, data classification, user count, SSO, SCIM and MFA status, last access, BAA or SOC 2 status and spend. Tiering applies Tier 0 to 3 by data sensitivity and blast radius: Tier 0 gets per-app deep work, lower tiers get baselined.

Baseline runs an SSPM, or the equivalent native tooling per Tier 0 app, against CIS, SCuBA or framework-aligned baselines, and generates a backlog. Remediation starts at the top: public shares, dormant accounts, MFA enforcement, admin roles and OAuth app review, some of which are operational, not one-shot.

OAuth app governance sets admin-consent policies, builds the review cadence and documents the revocation playbook. Continuous monitoring wires SSPM findings to ticketing, so drift becomes an alert rather than a quarterly discovery. ITDR detection forwards Tier 0 audit logs to the SIEM, with detections for the IdP, M365 or Workspace, Salesforce, GitHub and Slack.

IR playbooks per Tier 0 app say what a compromised tenant looks like, who you call, what evidence you collect and how you revoke tokens. Lifecycle covers onboarding (SSO, SCIM and a security review required, OAuth scopes reviewed) and offboarding (SCIM deprovisioning, tenant data export, account closure).

The teams that do it well treat SaaS security like vulnerability management: a continuous program with rotating per-app deep dives, not a project with an end date.

== Copilots turn stale permissions into exposure
layout: text
section: ai-saas
Copilots work within the permissions the user already holds, so years of broad sharing become discoverable through a plain-language prompt, at machine speed. Fix the underlying access first, then layer DLP on the AI features.
notes:
Every SaaS vendor is bolting an AI copilot onto its product, and every team is authorizing AI tools to reach into that SaaS on their behalf. The page says both trends expand the SaaS attack surface in ways an SSPM program now has to track as first-class posture.

M365 Copilot, Salesforce Einstein and Agentforce, Glean and their peers do not create new access; they operate within the permissions the user already holds. Microsoft is explicit that Copilot retrieves from the Graph and respects existing permissions, sharing settings and policies. The problem is that years of broad sharing links, broken inheritance and "Everyone except external users" sites were tolerable when finding a file meant knowing it existed. A copilot makes that latent exposure trivially discoverable through a plain-language prompt.

The page puts the fix in the underlying access rather than in an AI control. Remediate over-shared and stale files, tighten internal permissions to least privilege, and scope sensitivity labels before you scale a copilot. Then layer DLP on the AI features themselves, so labeled or regulated data is blocked from being summarized, quoted or re-shared through a prompt.

== OAuth token sprawl: the Salesloft Drift lesson
layout: checklist
section: ai-saas
Attackers stole OAuth tokens for the Drift integration and ran bulk SOQL queries to exfiltrate data from hundreds of Salesforce tenants. The token itself was the access.
check: Inventory every OAuth grant as a non-human identity
check: Enforce least scope on connected apps
check: Rotate and revoke aggressively; alert on high-privilege grants
check: Diligence the vendor's token storage and revocation posture
check: Require approval before any new AI app authorization
check: Feed integration API activity into detection engineering
notes:
AI tools and agents request broad OAuth scopes to your SaaS, and those grants are high-value, long-lived and usually invisible to security, because a business user clicked Allow. The page's reference case is the Salesloft Drift breach by UNC6395. Attackers stole OAuth tokens for the Drift integration and, impersonating a trusted app, ran bulk SOQL queries to exfiltrate data from hundreds of Salesforce tenants, with no MFA to bypass and no user interaction. Google's GTIG writeup and the FBI's FLASH alert, coordinated with CISA, both center on the connected-app OAuth grant as the failure point.

Inventory every grant into your critical SaaS, with app name, owner, scopes and last use, and treat the token as a non-human identity rather than an app setting. Deny broad read, write and admin scopes an AI tool does not demonstrably need. The page says an SSPM program that only checks tenant misconfiguration is now behind: AI copilots, connected AI apps and their grants belong in the posture inventory, with a CASB view of shadow AI alongside.

Drift's compromise became its customers' compromise, and a vendor's own subprocessors are your fourth-party exposure. Assume any token you issue can be stolen and replayed. Run periodic grant reviews to prune unused and over-scoped connections. Bulk SOQL or Graph reads, off-hours pulls and anomalous data volumes from a connected app are exactly the signal the Salesloft Drift victims lacked, and a zero trust posture never treats a trusted integration's token as inherently safe.

== AWS, Azure and GCP side by side
layout: table
section: comparison
row: Capability | AWS | Azure / M365 | GCP / Workspace
row: Identity provider | IAM Identity Center; not a general IdP | Entra ID | Cloud Identity
row: SaaS app control | No native CASB | Defender for Cloud Apps | Chrome Enterprise Premium, Google SecOps
row: Native SSPM | None (partners) | Defender for Cloud Apps features | Limited (partners)
row: Audit logs to | S3, EventBridge, Security Lake | Sentinel, Defender XDR | Google SecOps, BigQuery
For most enterprises native tooling is a starting point, and a dedicated SSPM plus CASB combination provides the depth.
notes:
SaaS is not the hyperscalers' core business, but each cloud has identity, CASB and SaaS-management capabilities that intersect with SaaS security. This is an excerpt of the page's table, which also covers identity governance, conditional access (IAM Identity Center session policies, which are limited, against Entra Conditional Access and Context-Aware Access) and ITDR-shaped detections, lists the Workspace alert center beside Chrome Enterprise Premium for SaaS app control, and adds Log Analytics as an Azure audit log destination.

The page explains the pattern. Microsoft's native coverage comes from M365 being a major SaaS estate in its own right, and from Defender for Cloud Apps being a real CASB and SSPM hybrid. AWS has no native CASB. GCP sits between the two, with Workspace controls and BeyondCorp filling much of the gap.

For most enterprises, native tooling is a starting point, and a dedicated SSPM plus CASB combination provides the depth.

== Maturity stages
layout: timeline
section: maturity
event: Stage 1: Aware | Partial SSO-driven inventory; OAuth apps not inventoried; IR improvised
event: Stage 2: Baselined | Reconciled inventory; SSPM on Tier 0; OAuth reviewed quarterly
event: Stage 3: Continuous | Drift alerts to the backlog; ITDR live; tested IR playbooks
event: Stage 4: Strategic | A product-roadmap input; quarterly purple teams; its own function
Most enterprises in 2026 are Stage 1 with pockets of Stage 2. Stage 3 is the realistic target for a mature program with a year or two of dedicated investment.
notes:
The page offers a four-stage model. At Stage 1, Aware, the inventory is partial and SSO-driven only, shadow IT is acknowledged but not measured, Tier 0 apps have MFA while lower tiers are inconsistent, native dashboards such as Secure Score, Workspace Security Center and Salesforce Health Check are checked occasionally, and IR for a SaaS incident would be improvised.

At Stage 2, Baselined, the inventory reconciles finance, SSO and DNS, SSPM runs against Tier 0 apps with CIS or SCuBA baselines, the OAuth inventory is reviewed quarterly, SSO and SCIM are enforced for Tier 0 and most Tier 1 apps, M365 and Workspace audit logs reach the SIEM, and dormant-account remediation runs on a cadence.

At Stage 3, Continuous, SSPM drift alerts feed the security backlog, ITDR detections are live for the IdP and Tier 0 apps, admin consent is policy, per-app IR playbooks are tested, CASB runs inline for major SaaS, and SaaS onboarding is gated at procurement.

At Stage 4, Strategic, SaaS security is a product-roadmap input, quarterly purple-team exercises run against M365, Workspace, Salesforce and GitHub, SaaS supply-chain risk monitoring is continuous, and the SaaS security team is its own function.

The page says Stage 3 is the realistic target for a mature security program with a year or two of dedicated SaaS-security investment.

== Common pitfalls
layout: list
section: pitfalls
The page lists three more: underestimating log licensing, confusing CASB with SSPM, and treating SaaS security as a one-off project.
item: Relying on SSO logs alone
item: No OAuth app review
item: No shadow IT discovery
item: SSPM bought but not configured to alert
item: No IR playbook for SaaS
item: Salesforce treated like just another SaaS
item: SCIM not enforced
notes:
SSO logs tell you about authentication and nothing about what happened inside the SaaS afterwards, so you need the per-app audit logs too. The page says every SaaS security incident assessment in the last three years includes a finding about an unreviewed OAuth grant, so stand up the inventory and the review cadence before anything else. An inventory built from the SSO catalog alone misses a large share of the SaaS in use.

An SSPM left in monitor mode accumulates findings in a dashboard nobody opens; findings need to flow to a ticket queue with an owner. A compromised M365 admin account is the wrong moment to work out how to revoke tokens, force password resets, audit recent OAuth grants and pull mailbox-access evidence, so pre-write the playbook. Salesforce's sharing model is unique, its blast radius enormous and its native logging paid-tier, and the page says it deserves an order of magnitude more depth than the average app. Without SCIM, manual deprovisioning misses apps and ex-employee access persists for months.

The other three: assuming the audit log is in your tenant tier when often it is not, buying CASB or SSPM and assuming it covers the other's job, and treating SaaS security as a one-off project when SaaS adoption is monotonic and the program is permanent staffing.

== Do I need SSPM if I already have a CASB?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider where each tool sits relative to the data path, and which you would buy first with budget for only one.
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== Most mature programs run both
layout: answer
section: faq
CASB is inline; SSPM is out of band. With budget for one, pick CASB first if the dominant risk is data exfil via uploads and downloads, and SSPM first if it is misconfiguration and OAuth sprawl.
notes:
Most mature programs run both. CASB is inline: DLP, access control and traffic-side enforcement. SSPM is out of band: configuration posture, OAuth inventory and admin-role audit.

They overlap at the edges, since most CASBs have an API mode that does some SSPM-like checks, but the page says the depth on individual SaaS apps in a focused SSPM almost always exceeds the depth in a CASB's API mode.

The page's decision tree, if you only have budget for one: pick CASB first if the dominant risk is data exfil via uploads and downloads, and pick SSPM first if the dominant risk is misconfiguration and OAuth sprawl.

== How do I get started if I have nothing in place?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider what you can learn before buying anything, using the admin consoles you already have.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== Start with discovery and OAuth
layout: answer
section: faq
Reconcile SSO, finance, DNS / SSE and email parsing into one inventory. In parallel, pull the M365 and Workspace OAuth app lists in the admin console and review the high-impact grants.
notes:
Start with discovery and OAuth. Run a discovery exercise that reconciles SSO, finance, DNS or SSE and email parsing into a single SaaS inventory. In parallel, pull the OAuth app list for M365 and Workspace, which you can do in the admin console without buying anything, and review the high-impact grants. The page says those two exercises together produce immediate findings and immediate value.

From there, buy an SSPM if the budget exists. If not, start with the CISA SCuBA baselines for M365 and Workspace and the CIS Benchmarks for the rest of the Tier 0 apps.
